<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter Ochs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1752">1752</a>–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter Ochs was a Basel politician and historian who drafted the constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>, imposed under French influence in 1798. Closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-directory">French Directory</a>, he sat in the republic's executive Directory until he was forced out in 1799. He later held office in the canton of Basel and wrote a history of the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-ochs.jpg" alt="Peter Ochs" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown, upload by sidonius 10:31, 15 December 2006 (UTC) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Ochs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ochs was born on 20 August 1752 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, into a family that claimed roots in the Basel aristocracy, and settled in Basel in 1769. In 1776 he obtained a doctorate of laws, became a magistrate and entered politics, gaining many useful contacts through his marriage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> was then a confederacy of self-governing cantons held together by a loose military alliance, with no central government, and the privileges of its wealthiest families bred resentment and uprisings, such as that of Major Abraham Davel against Bern's rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> broke out in 1789, Ochs joined the partisans of reform. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-cesar-de-la-harpe">Frédéric-César de La Harpe</a> he called on the French to send troops into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, overthrow what he called the decayed Confederation and found a unified nation. In 1798 French troops entered the country and took it over against little resistance. The constitution Ochs drew up abolished the Confederacy and set up a central government on the French model, with a two-chamber legislature.</p><p>Ochs served as the first president of the Helvetic Senate and later as president of the Directory, the state executive. He fell out with La Harpe and was forced out of government on 25 June 1799. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> itself lasted only until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a> of 1803, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> a confederacy once more.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Back in Basel, Ochs gained local standing for his part in drawing up new codes of government and of criminal law and in reorganising the city's university. He died in Basel on 19 June 1821. Peter-Ochs-Strasse in the city bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter Ochs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 12 April 1798 a constitution drafted by <b>Peter Ochs</b> of Basel proclaimed the Helvetic Republic, one and indivisible, with its first capital at Aarau.</blockquote><a href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Basel. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.22,-1.55&amp;year=1796" aria-label="Nantes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4514 507 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter Ochs"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4964 777h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4964 777h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.22,-1.55&amp;year=1796">What was here in 1796? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
